---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import PendulumSim from '../../simulators/pendulum/PendulumSim.astro';
import { pendulumQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/pendulum';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'How a pendulum works' },
  { id: 'period', label: 'What sets the period?' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Energy in a swing' },
  { id: 'experiment', label: 'Running a fair experiment' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Section 15.4: Pendulums. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-4-pendulums' },
  { text: 'Urone, P. P., & Hinrichs, R. (2020). College Physics 2e, Section 16.4: The Simple Pendulum. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/16-4-the-simple-pendulum' },
  { text: 'Nelson, R. A., & Olsson, M. G. (1986). The pendulum — Rich physics from a simple system. American Journal of Physics, 54(2), 112–121.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="physics"
  topic="pendulum"
  description="Interactive pendulum simulator: change length, mass, angle, gravity and friction, measure the period, watch energy change and record your own data."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={pendulumQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A weight on a string, swinging back and forth: the pendulum is one of the simplest machines in physics, yet it
      kept the world’s time for nearly 300 years. Change one variable at a time, measure the period, and discover which
      factors really control the swing — and which do not.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <PendulumSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong> to release the bob. The <strong>measured period</strong> appears after the first full swing.</li>
      <li>Change <strong>one variable at a time</strong> — length, mass, starting angle, gravity or friction. Each change restarts the swing.</li>
      <li>To set the angle by hand, <strong>pause</strong> and <strong>drag the bob</strong> to either side.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Record</strong> to save the current measurement in the table. Download your results as a CSV file to graph them in a spreadsheet.</li>
      <li>Watch the <strong>energy bars</strong> and the <strong>angle graph</strong> as the pendulum swings. Click the bob or string for more information.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">How a pendulum works</h2>
  <p>
    When the bob is pulled to one side, gravity pulls it down while the string holds it on a circular arc. The part of
    gravity that acts along the arc, called the <strong>restoring force</strong>, always points back toward the lowest
    point. The bob speeds up as it falls, overshoots the bottom because of its inertia, slows as it climbs the other side,
    and swings back. This repeating motion is an <strong>oscillation</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="period">What sets the period?</h2>
  <p>
    Only two things matter much for a pendulum with small swings: the <strong>length</strong> of the string and the
    <strong>strength of gravity</strong>. A longer string gives a longer period; stronger gravity gives a shorter one.
    The <strong>mass</strong> of the bob makes no difference, because a heavier bob needs more force to accelerate and
    receives exactly that much more force from gravity. The <strong>starting angle</strong> matters only a little:
    for swings under about 15°, the period is almost the same whatever the amplitude. Galileo noticed this property,
    called <strong>isochronism</strong>, in the early 1600s.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <p>For small angles, the period of a simple pendulum is:</p>
  <code class="formula">T = 2π √(L / g)        frequency f = 1 / T</code>
  <p>
    The formula comes from the equation of motion θ'' = −(g/L) sin θ, using the approximation sin θ ≈ θ for small
    angles. The simulator solves the full equation without this approximation, which is why its measured period
    grows slightly at large angles. A good correction for moderate angles θ₀ (in radians) is:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">T ≈ 2π √(L/g) · (1 + θ₀²/16)</code>

  <h2 id="energy">Energy in a swing</h2>
  <p>
    At the highest points the bob is momentarily still: all its energy is <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong>
    (PE = m·g·h). At the lowest point it moves fastest: the energy is all <strong>kinetic</strong> (KE = ½·m·v²). In
    between, energy flows back and forth while the total stays constant — unless friction is switched on, in which case
    the total slowly drains away as heat and the swings shrink.
  </p>

  <h2 id="experiment">Running a fair experiment</h2>
  <p>
    A scientific investigation changes one <strong>independent variable</strong> at a time, measures a
    <strong>dependent variable</strong> (here, the period) and keeps all other <strong>control variables</strong> the
    same. Try recording five lengths at the same angle and mass, then plot T against √L: the points form a straight
    line, confirming the formula.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Period (T)</dt>
    <dd>Time for one complete oscillation, in seconds.</dd>
    <dt>Frequency (f)</dt>
    <dd>Number of oscillations per second, in hertz (Hz). f = 1/T.</dd>
    <dt>Amplitude</dt>
    <dd>The maximum displacement from the rest position — here, the starting angle.</dd>
    <dt>Restoring force</dt>
    <dd>A force that pulls a system back toward equilibrium; for a pendulum, m·g·sin θ.</dd>
    <dt>Simple harmonic motion</dt>
    <dd>Oscillation in which the restoring force is proportional to displacement. A pendulum approximates it for small angles.</dd>
    <dt>Damping</dt>
    <dd>Loss of energy (for example to air friction) that makes oscillations die away.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clocks:</strong> pendulum clocks, invented by Christiaan Huygens in 1656, were the most accurate timekeepers until the 20th century. A pendulum about 0.99 m long has a period of 2 s — the classic “seconds pendulum”.</li>
    <li><strong>Measuring gravity:</strong> because T depends on g, precise pendulums were used to map small variations in Earth’s gravity.</li>
    <li><strong>Foucault’s pendulum:</strong> a long, heavy pendulum whose swing plane slowly rotates, demonstrating that Earth turns.</li>
    <li><strong>Buildings and bridges:</strong> tuned mass dampers — giant pendulum-like weights — reduce swaying in skyscrapers such as Taipei 101.</li>
    <li><strong>Walking:</strong> your legs swing partly like pendulums, which is one reason taller people tend to take slower strides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“A heavier bob swings faster.”</strong> Mass does not change the period.</li>
    <li><strong>“A bigger swing takes much longer.”</strong> For small angles, the period hardly changes with amplitude: a bigger swing also moves faster.</li>
    <li><strong>“Friction changes the period a lot.”</strong> Light damping shrinks the amplitude but changes the period very little.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The model is an ideal <em>simple pendulum</em>: a point mass on a massless, unstretchable string, swinging in one
    plane. Real pendulums have a bob with size and a string with mass, which slightly lengthen the period. Air friction is
    modelled as a simple force proportional to speed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>For small swings, T = 2π√(L/g): the period depends on length and gravity only.</li>
    <li>Mass has no effect on the period; amplitude has only a small effect at large angles.</li>
    <li>Energy changes between potential and kinetic during each swing.</li>
    <li>Good experiments change one variable at a time and repeat measurements.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
